179
00:15:31,298 --> 00:15:40,964
[SPEAKER_03]: of a mind that's liable to suffering, you would remove the necessary basis or the fuel that was causing that suffering.

180
00:15:41,504 --> 00:15:50,189
[SPEAKER_03]: It might not disappear right away, but when you're aware of your liability to suffering, you won't be doing certain things anymore.

181
00:15:51,287 --> 00:15:54,808
[SPEAKER_03]: You can just take it on that on that very general level.

182
00:15:55,089 --> 00:16:08,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's because of ignoring the fact that I'm still alive and suffering, this problem is still here, that you become heedless and just do things that undermine your own goal.

183
00:16:40,176 --> 00:16:42,296
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, let's let's try to explain that then.

184
00:16:42,637 --> 00:16:47,298
[SPEAKER_03]: What would be the difference between... because it could easily just be an abstract fact.

185
00:16:47,538 --> 00:16:49,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I just sit down and I say, okay, I was suffering.

186
00:16:50,939 --> 00:16:56,922
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just kind of like a... again, on the same level as as like a Saturn has X amount of rings.

187
00:16:57,842 --> 00:16:58,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

188
00:17:07,142 --> 00:17:15,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes an actual foundation of mindfulness, as the phrase says.

189
00:17:25,131 --> 00:17:27,553
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a liability, suffering, something that you just reason.

190
00:17:30,394 --> 00:17:31,235
[SPEAKER_04]: Or it might be called...

191
00:17:35,254 --> 00:17:40,318
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not concrete in the sense of something that you can focus on.

192
00:17:40,558 --> 00:17:41,579
[SPEAKER_04]: So it's got...

193
00:17:43,540 --> 00:17:44,080
[SPEAKER_04]: I want to say...

194
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:45,622
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a sensation or something.

195
00:17:45,982 --> 00:17:46,802
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

196
00:17:50,065 --> 00:17:52,226
[SPEAKER_04]: But I mean, it's present.

197
00:17:52,507 --> 00:17:53,467
[SPEAKER_04]: But what is it?

198
00:17:59,602 --> 00:18:00,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Just in colloquial terms.

199
00:18:00,823 --> 00:18:01,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

200
00:18:01,283 --> 00:18:07,547
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, I want to say it's like a knowledge essence.

201
00:18:07,607 --> 00:18:08,508
[SPEAKER_04]: Just as a word.

202
00:18:09,068 --> 00:18:12,030
[SPEAKER_04]: You know that you're liable to suffering.

203
00:18:12,630 --> 00:18:12,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

204
00:18:13,170 --> 00:18:14,411
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is something more.

205
00:18:16,072 --> 00:18:18,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that knowledge can also still be abstract.

206
00:18:18,594 --> 00:18:18,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

207
00:18:19,234 --> 00:18:19,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

208
00:18:19,414 --> 00:18:27,079
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the difference between the knowledge that you now refer to and just telling yourself, I'm still liable to suffering?

209
00:18:27,099 --> 00:18:28,280
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody could just get convinced.

210
00:18:29,335 --> 00:18:37,819
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, in the same way that they're convinced of the level of external knowledge, I am liable to suffering, they could convince themselves I am not liable to suffering.

211
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:49,144
[SPEAKER_03]: Just based on, like, that you're given a list, this is what it means to be liable to suffering, and you check all the boxes, okay, that's it.

212
00:18:49,284 --> 00:18:52,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Somebody gives you a different list, turns out you're not liable to suffering.

213
00:18:52,666 --> 00:18:56,068
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's why you can't convince someone to practice the dhammas.

214
00:18:57,213 --> 00:18:58,134
[SPEAKER_00]: They have to see that.

215
00:18:58,294 --> 00:19:00,035
[SPEAKER_00]: They have to see that, like, really, but what is it?

216
00:19:03,338 --> 00:19:06,761
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, isn't this the same question as what is suffering?

217
00:19:08,342 --> 00:19:09,383
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, in a way, you could say.

218
00:19:09,523 --> 00:19:10,904
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is fine.

219
00:19:11,925 --> 00:19:14,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in relation to what do you most acutely suffer?

220
00:19:15,668 --> 00:19:16,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings.

221
00:19:16,589 --> 00:19:17,009
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

222
00:19:17,189 --> 00:19:17,469
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

223
00:19:19,411 --> 00:19:22,013
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, on account of unpleasant feelings.

224
00:19:24,075 --> 00:19:24,435
[SPEAKER_04]: Feelings.

225
00:19:25,951 --> 00:19:28,713
[SPEAKER_03]: But is it like the liability itself?

226
00:19:29,894 --> 00:19:32,056
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it that it's on account of unpleasant feelings?

227
00:19:32,637 --> 00:19:36,640
[SPEAKER_03]: In a way, you could say that, but isn't the liability itself a feeling as well?

228
00:19:37,541 --> 00:19:38,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the thing.

229
00:19:38,662 --> 00:19:45,488
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to mystify like, oh, yeah, the liability to suffering is a kind of abstract entity.

230
00:19:45,568 --> 00:19:45,828
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

231
00:19:45,868 --> 00:19:47,710
[SPEAKER_02]: It's something that you feel as well.

232
00:19:50,252 --> 00:19:53,515
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I find it's hard to think about suffering when you're

233
00:19:55,898 --> 00:19:57,559
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

234
00:19:58,059 --> 00:20:00,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, generally, feeling bad.

235
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:00,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

236
00:20:01,140 --> 00:20:06,222
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is going to be something that you can look at readily.

237
00:20:06,322 --> 00:20:06,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

238
00:20:06,502 --> 00:20:07,462
[SPEAKER_03]: The thing is, it's there.

239
00:20:07,542 --> 00:20:09,943
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you're not used to looking in that direction.

240
00:20:10,123 --> 00:20:10,543
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it...

241
00:20:10,763 --> 00:20:17,125
[SPEAKER_03]: But you deep down know that if certain things were to happen, like, it's already there.

242
00:20:17,406 --> 00:20:20,987
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of vulnerability, if you can put it that way.

243
00:20:21,107 --> 00:20:21,807
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already there.

244
00:20:23,008 --> 00:20:23,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you kind of

245
00:20:24,558 --> 00:20:44,890
[SPEAKER_03]: Realize only once the things happen, because if if something, let's say, something that you, that is dear to you, were to suddenly be in danger, and you find out, it's not like after it goes away, after the thing, you lose it, then you will realize, oh, I'm suffering.

246
00:20:45,570 --> 00:20:49,513
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's like immediately, when you find out this might happen,

247
00:20:50,778 --> 00:20:51,538
[SPEAKER_00]: You already know.

248
00:20:51,859 --> 00:20:53,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, basically, suffering is already there.

249
00:20:53,800 --> 00:21:00,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which does mean that the more comfortable somebody's life is, and the more they generally get what they want, the harder it is to see this.

250
00:21:00,904 --> 00:21:12,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they now need to make a deliberate effort in order to see that their good feelings and their moods depend on a host of things being there, taken for granted.

251
00:21:13,253 --> 00:21:15,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're all, without exception, impermanent.

252
00:21:15,475 --> 00:21:23,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't see that, and you wait for that to fall apart, and only then do you realize that you have a problem, you have entirely overlooked the liability.

253
00:21:23,383 --> 00:21:23,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

254
00:21:23,804 --> 00:21:32,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's quite common for people to be skeptical of the message of the Buddha and the Four Noble Truths, saying, what's all this talk about suffering now, the whole time?

255
00:21:32,472 --> 00:21:32,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Mostly.

256
00:21:33,213 --> 00:21:35,095
[SPEAKER_01]: But, generally speaking, I'm doing quite okay.

257
00:21:35,666 --> 00:21:37,387
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they begin to rationalize just there.

258
00:21:37,427 --> 00:21:43,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, okay, I understand 50 years from now I might be old, I might get sick, but I'll deal with it then.

259
00:21:43,912 --> 00:21:45,534
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a sort of common rationalization.

260
00:21:46,174 --> 00:21:52,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that now all sorts of things can happen and are happening that you are already running away from.

261
00:21:52,559 --> 00:21:53,640
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not even seeing that then.

262
00:21:55,101 --> 00:22:00,325
[SPEAKER_03]: Realistically, you have to kind of bring it to the surface by thinking about certain things.

263
00:22:01,133 --> 00:22:09,058
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, it might even feel to you like, okay, I start suffering only when I think about death, or only when I think about aging, only when I think about sickness.

264
00:22:09,818 --> 00:22:12,039
[SPEAKER_03]: From your initial perspective, it might seem that way.

265
00:22:12,760 --> 00:22:20,845
[SPEAKER_03]: But you realize, the only reason why these reflections were able to trigger suffering, is because you were already in a state of suffering.

266
00:22:20,885 --> 00:22:25,327
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just not as course as you're used to.

267
00:22:25,367 --> 00:22:30,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't have these specific signs that you expect.

268
00:22:31,972 --> 00:22:38,839
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you want to refine those recognitions and see, like, even the liability to suffering, the fact that nothing is happening right now,

269
00:22:54,911 --> 00:22:57,073
[SPEAKER_03]: But anything, anything at all, could happen.

270
00:22:57,153 --> 00:22:59,875
[SPEAKER_03]: It could upset my mind to any extent.

271
00:23:00,075 --> 00:23:02,117
[SPEAKER_03]: That's suffering in itself, because you're not safe.

272
00:23:03,438 --> 00:23:07,542
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like... it's not something that you can entirely forget about.

273
00:23:07,782 --> 00:23:10,224
[SPEAKER_03]: Your entire existence revolves around it.

274
00:23:10,244 --> 00:23:17,070
[SPEAKER_03]: So, whatever you're doing, no matter how small, is somehow related to the fact that you're vulnerable to suffering.

275
00:23:17,550 --> 00:23:20,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and so hope and fear is another indication of this liability.

276
00:23:22,080 --> 00:23:25,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why would you either fear or hope?

277
00:23:25,922 --> 00:23:28,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Hope and fear go together, but why would you hope for or against something?

278
00:23:28,964 --> 00:23:32,606
[SPEAKER_01]: That's only because you know that you are still liable to suffering.

279
00:23:33,106 --> 00:23:41,210
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't make that connection, you can say hope is a good thing, and then failing to see that as a sign of more work to be done.

280
00:23:41,911 --> 00:23:48,674
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's... you emphasize, let's say, the positive aspect of, yeah, when this thing that I want comes about.

281
00:23:49,215 --> 00:23:51,416
[SPEAKER_03]: Failing to see that you wanting that thing,

282
00:23:52,366 --> 00:23:53,447
[SPEAKER_03]: is the first problem.

283
00:23:53,827 --> 00:23:54,127
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

284
00:23:54,187 --> 00:23:57,669
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, whether you're able to get it or not, that's now a separate thing.

285
00:23:57,709 --> 00:23:57,969
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

286
00:24:13,859 --> 00:24:20,623
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that means being mindful of your liability to suffering, is being mindful of your feelings, or being mindful of your mind.

287
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:35,335
[SPEAKER_03]: But of course, like, because of the whole perversion of the views, people would put all the emphasis on the body.

288
00:24:36,296 --> 00:24:48,323
[SPEAKER_03]: And, eh... like, for myself, I've rarely ever heard anybody speak about how you're mindful of your feelings, or how you're mindful of the mind, unless they can play feelings or sensations to make it much more palpable.

289
00:24:49,043 --> 00:24:52,225
[SPEAKER_03]: But that whole dimension of how you feel,

290
00:24:53,627 --> 00:25:00,851
[SPEAKER_03]: Without making it kind of objectified into sensations, it's almost impossible for people to see.

291
00:25:01,912 --> 00:25:12,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it is impossible for people to see, unless they've developed themselves in virtue, like not acting out of certain things, which are already rooted in all those feelings and mental states.

292
00:25:13,318 --> 00:25:22,163
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you don't act out of them, it's like the dust settles, let's say, and you're able to see, oh, this is what would have led me to act out.

293
00:25:22,868 --> 00:25:23,689
[SPEAKER_03]: And it becomes clear.

294
00:25:23,729 --> 00:25:34,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, even in the suttas, you would see all the time, when people would come to ask the Buddha for advice on practicing mindfulness, he would start by saying you have to practice virtue.

295
00:25:35,858 --> 00:25:41,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, when for you, mindfulness is just observation, especially on the level of sensations,

296
00:25:42,435 --> 00:25:44,736
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be... almost seem like unconnected.

297
00:25:44,836 --> 00:25:45,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why do I have to...

298
00:25:46,356 --> 00:25:48,176
[SPEAKER_00]: I could just start watching my sensations right now.

299
00:25:48,356 --> 00:25:59,559
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, that's evidence of the fact that what the Buddha is referring to as body is not what you think body is when you can practice mindfulness of it without virtue.

300
00:26:04,841 --> 00:26:06,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's in that same direction.

301
00:26:06,281 --> 00:26:07,661
[SPEAKER_00]: The body that matters, basically.

302
00:26:07,741 --> 00:26:10,842
[SPEAKER_03]: It's that direction that's underneath.

303
00:26:11,241 --> 00:26:16,044
[SPEAKER_03]: The same way your mental state is not something that you can kind of pinpoint.

304
00:26:16,825 --> 00:26:19,846
[SPEAKER_03]: The body that you're supposed to be mindful of is on that same level.

305
00:26:19,986 --> 00:26:25,550
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the body that undermines your ownership, not the body that you are relating to with your ownership.

306
00:26:32,872 --> 00:26:33,832
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even the body, really.

307
00:26:33,872 --> 00:26:36,513
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just kind of... yeah, perceptions.

308
00:26:36,613 --> 00:26:44,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not even more... any more special, as I've said before, than just sounds and smells.

309
00:26:44,236 --> 00:26:47,677
[SPEAKER_00]: That nobody tries to observe their body by smelling things.

310
00:26:48,498 --> 00:26:49,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't make sense.

311
00:26:50,678 --> 00:26:50,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

312
00:26:50,879 --> 00:26:55,700
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility of ownership is also there only because of that more fundamental body.

313
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:56,180
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

314
00:26:56,801 --> 00:27:00,342
[SPEAKER_03]: The possibility of seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting, touching,

315
00:27:00,890 --> 00:27:07,255
[SPEAKER_03]: Even thinking is there because of that body that you overlook and you descend onto the sense objects.

316
00:27:09,797 --> 00:27:18,963
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you... that's why then, if you establish it, you wouldn't need to keep focusing on it, because sight, sound, smell, taste, touches are all secondary to it.

317
00:27:19,364 --> 00:27:20,124
[SPEAKER_03]: That was the whole point.

318
00:27:20,204 --> 00:27:28,290
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you need to keep making your... like returning to the perceptions of it, as if those perceptions are it.

319
00:27:44,823 --> 00:28:05,721
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you have recognized it to some extent, any of these things, the body, or liability to suffering, or hindrance, let's say, because that's also included, it doesn't say that you kind of... immediately, when you know a hindrance, the first thing you do is replace it.

320
00:28:06,182 --> 00:28:07,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Just think about something else.

321
00:28:07,363 --> 00:28:09,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, that's part of the satipaṭṭhāna.

322
00:28:09,745 --> 00:28:10,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing the hindrances,

323
00:28:11,152 --> 00:28:14,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing how they arise, how they cease.

324
00:28:15,576 --> 00:28:26,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you, once you establish some context, prevent yourself from falling away from it, without constantly bringing it to mind?

325
00:28:40,450 --> 00:28:46,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Realistically, you are gonna fall away from that, when you figure it out, the lecture ends.

326
00:28:47,787 --> 00:28:59,349
[SPEAKER_02]: Your ability to not lose track of it is going to be, probably, proportionally related to how familiar you are with bringing yourself back to the point where you can't recognize it.

327
00:28:59,689 --> 00:29:00,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

328
00:29:00,429 --> 00:29:04,230
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... Yeah, that still doesn't answer the question.

329
00:29:04,250 --> 00:29:04,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

330
00:29:04,650 --> 00:29:06,951
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't let you fall away from it.

331
00:29:06,971 --> 00:29:08,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you... Yeah.

332
00:29:08,531 --> 00:29:10,151
[SPEAKER_00]: What causes you to fall away from it, basically?

333
00:29:10,171 --> 00:29:11,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, eh...

334
00:29:13,672 --> 00:29:14,052
[SPEAKER_00]: It's...

335
00:29:20,052 --> 00:29:24,176
[SPEAKER_05]: When you fall away from it, it's because you didn't see yourself acting out of it.

336
00:29:24,536 --> 00:29:28,379
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's just like this lack of recognition, of just jumping into the content.

337
00:29:28,919 --> 00:29:31,681
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you basically act out and you didn't see it, basically.

338
00:29:32,242 --> 00:29:36,625
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you fall away from it because you were a bit careless.

339
00:29:36,965 --> 00:29:38,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, but, so...

340
00:29:40,258 --> 00:29:41,439
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you respond to that?

341
00:29:41,479 --> 00:29:45,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you just determine harder next time that I will not distract myself?

342
00:29:45,902 --> 00:29:46,402
[SPEAKER_05]: No, no.

343
00:29:46,422 --> 00:29:51,265
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to recognize, like, as somebody said, like, more clear where the line of your responsibility is.

344
00:29:51,285 --> 00:29:54,047
[SPEAKER_05]: It's kind of indirect, but in a way, it's very direct as well.

345
00:29:54,107 --> 00:29:54,927
[SPEAKER_03]: It's kind of... Yeah.

346
00:29:56,208 --> 00:30:09,657
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm saying that because, naturally, yes, you could recognize I fell away from it because I chose to attend to something else, but you also want to recognize it cannot be forced.

347
00:30:10,519 --> 00:30:12,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you cannot just, as a matter of...

348
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:14,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I decide now that I will not fall away from the content.

349
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:26,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that thing that is making you fall away, practically speaking, is so close to you that you won't be able to, initially at least, see it.

350
00:30:26,223 --> 00:30:30,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, here's the option to distract myself, so I won't choose it.

351
00:30:30,925 --> 00:30:31,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

352
00:30:32,877 --> 00:30:46,348
[SPEAKER_00]: As a result of persistence, of noticing yourself falling back, and then bringing it back again, that's how you become less liable to falling away, because it's being reinforced.

353
00:30:46,388 --> 00:30:53,614
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, really, the reason why you fall away, even more accurately speaking, is because you still have an assumption.

354
00:30:55,035 --> 00:31:01,200
[SPEAKER_00]: You still have an assumption that allows for you to believe that the body isn't actually there.

355
00:31:02,075 --> 00:31:14,841
[SPEAKER_00]: When you go to think about this or that, that the feeling is not there anymore, when you kind of distract yourself with entertainment or something that's going to take you away.

356
00:31:14,861 --> 00:31:21,103
[SPEAKER_05]: Can we say, like, maybe a bit... maybe not... a bit abstract maybe, but can we say, like, falling away is what you are?

357
00:31:22,204 --> 00:31:22,764
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

358
00:31:23,124 --> 00:31:27,046
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, actually, that's why it says, eh... based on assumption, you have being.

359
00:31:28,687 --> 00:31:31,868
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't fall away from the right perspective,

360
00:31:32,501 --> 00:31:38,605
[SPEAKER_00]: You could see, at the very least, I may not be completely free from being, but that's the direction where being will increase.

361
00:31:40,066 --> 00:31:55,397
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a sutta where, I think it's Majjhima too, where it says like, eh... understanding yoniso manasikāra means understanding how your attention, the things you attend to, lead to the increase

362
00:31:56,473 --> 00:32:01,095
[SPEAKER_00]: of the influxes of sensuality, being, and ignorance.

363
00:32:01,115 --> 00:32:05,497
[SPEAKER_00]: I think people read that line and it might seem kind of abstract, but it's factually that.

364
00:32:05,758 --> 00:32:11,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you see being as a very kind of broad phenomenon.

365
00:32:12,021 --> 00:32:16,683
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like the... you don't have like a sensation or an object of being.

366
00:32:17,623 --> 00:32:25,347
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a whole kind of overall situation that comes to be on account of you

367
00:32:26,912 --> 00:32:35,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Emphasizing the content at the expense of that foundation, background foundation, that you have recognized.

368
00:32:35,555 --> 00:32:42,037
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's say you are established in the knowledge that you are right now liable to suffering.

369
00:32:42,077 --> 00:32:44,437
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a mind that's factually liable to suffering.

370
00:32:44,597 --> 00:32:48,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as a natural idea, but something that... a feeling there that you have recognized.

371
00:32:50,519 --> 00:32:52,760
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what's going to happen?

372
00:33:01,351 --> 00:33:05,993
[SPEAKER_04]: If you don't undermine that liability, that liability will be what is hooked.

373
00:33:07,134 --> 00:33:10,295
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's your assumptions.

374
00:33:11,536 --> 00:33:12,536
[SPEAKER_03]: No, but let's say you saw it.

375
00:33:12,576 --> 00:33:15,478
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you're no longer kind of overlooking it.

376
00:33:16,638 --> 00:33:21,580
[SPEAKER_03]: So, let's say, for now, the assumption is not there, as strongly, at least, as it was before.

377
00:33:22,501 --> 00:33:25,042
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what happens next, basically?

378
00:33:30,698 --> 00:33:32,039
[SPEAKER_05]: You ask if you need to do something else.

379
00:33:32,260 --> 00:33:32,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

380
00:33:33,100 --> 00:33:36,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, because... like, is it going to stop there?

381
00:33:36,804 --> 00:33:38,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you just remember it and that's it.

382
00:33:38,505 --> 00:33:39,947
[SPEAKER_00]: My mind is established now.

383
00:33:42,389 --> 00:33:42,849
[SPEAKER_00]: You saw it?

384
00:33:43,850 --> 00:33:44,691
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there, factually.

385
00:33:46,352 --> 00:33:47,053
[SPEAKER_00]: But what happens

386
00:33:52,088 --> 00:33:54,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, that could be one option.

387
00:33:55,209 --> 00:33:56,109
[SPEAKER_00]: That could be one option.

388
00:33:56,169 --> 00:33:58,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Or let's say doubt.

389
00:33:58,510 --> 00:34:01,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say, even before it becomes panic, let's say you have doubt.

390
00:34:01,471 --> 00:34:03,011
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess that's more what I mean, more so.

391
00:34:03,151 --> 00:34:09,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like, let's say, you're feeling like, okay, but is this really the liability?

392
00:34:09,093 --> 00:34:10,313
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you saw it.

393
00:34:10,674 --> 00:34:11,514
[SPEAKER_03]: It was a feeling of

394
00:34:13,004 --> 00:34:21,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Uncertainty, vulnerability, it's like, well, maybe if I do something, I can make this liability go away quicker or something.

395
00:34:21,943 --> 00:34:24,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Or any sort of direction that the mind might try...

396
00:34:28,720 --> 00:34:29,760
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, so that's the thing.

397
00:34:29,780 --> 00:34:33,362
[SPEAKER_05]: That was what I was aiming at with the question.

398
00:34:33,402 --> 00:34:36,163
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, it will never feel like it's complete.

399
00:34:49,463 --> 00:35:04,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind will keep trying to convince you that you need to do more, you need to increase the awareness or something, and that's now the bait that's pulling you back into the content, even if it feels completely justified.

400
00:35:05,791 --> 00:35:11,754
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you would fall back into... like, you will increase your being from where it's at now.

401
00:35:14,616 --> 00:35:15,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And all the...

402
00:35:21,353 --> 00:35:31,602
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't engage with that content that the mind is trying to distract you with, you will find that it becomes harder and harder to go back to that background that you discerned initially.

403
00:35:32,463 --> 00:35:38,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the Buddha would say it's like a... like a... was it a quail or something?

404
00:35:38,228 --> 00:35:43,973
[SPEAKER_03]: That should... in regards to the satipaṭṭhāna, it's like it goes into kind of like a hole.

405
00:35:44,974 --> 00:35:52,900
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the hawk comes and tries to get it, but then it can't because, of course, it's a hawk.

406
00:35:53,721 --> 00:35:55,502
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the whole point.

407
00:35:55,562 --> 00:36:01,327
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not things for you to kind of just lose yourself in and just forget about everything else.

408
00:36:01,387 --> 00:36:04,249
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole point is that things will be coming up, trying to

409
00:36:05,268 --> 00:36:06,129
[SPEAKER_03]: Take you away from it.

410
00:36:06,809 --> 00:36:14,536
[SPEAKER_03]: And, eh... you want to see... you don't want to be kind of fighting those things by just, no, no, I won't think about these things.

411
00:36:14,576 --> 00:36:17,919
[SPEAKER_03]: I'll just think about the... the... the body or the feeling.

412
00:36:18,440 --> 00:36:20,422
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, they're not even on the same level.

413
00:36:20,722 --> 00:36:23,364
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you cannot just replace one with the other.

414
00:36:24,085 --> 00:36:31,912
[SPEAKER_03]: Simply by staying with that recognition, that background, that pretty much undermines all of these things already,

415
00:36:32,994 --> 00:36:37,119
[SPEAKER_03]: They become irrelevant on their own, without you having to kind of push them away.

416
00:36:37,400 --> 00:36:40,403
[SPEAKER_03]: Because pushing them away would be another way of you involving yourself with them.

417
00:36:41,124 --> 00:36:50,516
[SPEAKER_03]: If you saw that basis of your experience rightly, it wouldn't occur to you to involve yourself with these things, because it's just like...

418
00:36:51,155 --> 00:36:58,678
[SPEAKER_03]: Kind of falling down from a higher place when you were already safer than you were when you engaged.

419
00:36:59,859 --> 00:37:12,464
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, it's about sinking, kind of hammering in the fact that even though it doesn't feel that way, even though there is still the assumption that the safety will come when I resolve the content,

420
00:37:13,055 --> 00:37:16,037
[SPEAKER_03]: When I have the answer that I'm looking for... No.

421
00:37:16,378 --> 00:37:24,143
[SPEAKER_03]: The safety is when I stay here and just, even if these things come up, I see through them.

422
00:37:24,444 --> 00:37:25,564
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, I see them.

423
00:37:26,485 --> 00:37:27,486
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't just push them away.

424
00:37:27,886 --> 00:37:31,569
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't just kind of pretend that they don't exist.

425
00:37:32,450 --> 00:37:39,355
[SPEAKER_03]: I see them in relation to this thing that I'm established in and I prioritize that, not the things.

426
00:37:58,132 --> 00:38:08,500
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what it means, like, you... by practicing satipaṭṭhāna, you give up covetousness and grief, as it's often translated, in regards to the world.

427
00:38:08,520 --> 00:38:10,142
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't say, oh, you just forget the world.

428
00:38:11,963 --> 00:38:12,824
[SPEAKER_03]: It will keep coming up.

429
00:38:13,284 --> 00:38:13,705
[SPEAKER_03]: It has to.

430
00:38:14,805 --> 00:38:25,974
[SPEAKER_03]: But, through this, you purify yourself from any tendency to just lean in that direction, carelessly, again, and just...

431
00:38:27,022 --> 00:38:30,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Almost by habit, prioritize anything.

432
00:38:32,047 --> 00:38:37,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's past, present, future, here, there, other people, yourself.

433
00:38:38,935 --> 00:38:40,476
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to deny any of those things.

434
00:38:41,297 --> 00:38:47,584
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to make sure that you never put them first over the background, the foundation.

435
00:38:59,837 --> 00:39:10,520
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, like, now, even just the word foundation makes sense, but I remember when I first started to practice, like, meditation and stuff, they were talking about, like, foundation of mindfulness and stuff, and it was like... Why is the word foundation?

436
00:39:10,540 --> 00:39:13,261
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, like, why don't you look at your knee, not, like, look at the tree or something?

437
00:39:13,321 --> 00:39:15,241
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, why is that this one is a foundation?

438
00:39:15,261 --> 00:39:16,822
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

439
00:39:17,722 --> 00:39:18,742
[SPEAKER_05]: It's quite... Yeah.

440
00:39:22,563 --> 00:39:28,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, actually, I think it's not an exaggeration to say that people basically ignore the other three.

441
00:39:31,257 --> 00:39:33,178
[SPEAKER_00]: At best, it's like a theoretical explanation.

442
00:39:33,758 --> 00:39:36,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, when you're in your meditation, you examine the dhammas or something.

443
00:39:37,059 --> 00:39:37,359
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

444
00:39:38,279 --> 00:39:39,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But not before that.

445
00:39:39,860 --> 00:39:40,920
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just see dhammas.

446
00:39:41,380 --> 00:39:41,620
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

447
00:39:41,780 --> 00:39:46,502
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, unless it kind of goes into, like, you get to the point where you're so advanced.

448
00:39:46,522 --> 00:39:47,062
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

449
00:39:47,082 --> 00:39:49,703
[SPEAKER_02]: Watching the body that you can then start to watch your mind.

450
00:39:49,723 --> 00:39:50,903
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

451
00:39:50,923 --> 00:39:51,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Or feelings.

452
00:39:51,924 --> 00:39:52,144
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

453
00:39:52,464 --> 00:39:53,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas it's the other way around.

454
00:39:54,044 --> 00:39:58,766
[SPEAKER_00]: That sutta of simile of the cook says, unless you have grasped the sign of the mind, you will be doing satipaṭ

455
00:40:01,917 --> 00:40:03,498
[SPEAKER_05]: Unless you see your money, you can't see the money.

456
00:40:03,538 --> 00:40:04,518
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

457
00:40:04,558 --> 00:40:06,259
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

458
00:40:07,540 --> 00:40:11,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you are composed in virtue and have the right view, you cannot develop satipaṭṭhāna.

459
00:40:11,202 --> 00:40:11,902
[SPEAKER_01]: It also says that.

460
00:40:11,922 --> 00:40:12,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

461
00:40:12,422 --> 00:40:22,448
[SPEAKER_02]: I think there's something to say for the fact that, like, to an extent, some of these ideas won't be

462
00:40:27,725 --> 00:40:40,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's not entirely obvious to me that acting out is like a kind of trying to step outside of the experience as a whole.

463
00:40:41,797 --> 00:40:43,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's not how it's gonna feel.

464
00:40:43,278 --> 00:40:43,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

465
00:40:43,598 --> 00:40:47,681
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see it as such, because can you actually step outside of the experience as a whole?

466
00:40:47,701 --> 00:40:49,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you were aware of that

467
00:40:50,415 --> 00:40:53,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that idea, you'd say, like, no, this is not actually what I'm doing.

468
00:40:53,416 --> 00:40:56,317
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but it just seems like I'm stepping somewhere else in the experience.

469
00:40:56,337 --> 00:40:56,657
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, yes.

470
00:40:57,118 --> 00:41:00,239
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, well, that's the assumption that you don't realize.

471
00:41:00,399 --> 00:41:04,460
[SPEAKER_03]: If you were to be aware of that assumption, you wouldn't be able to have it, because it's ridiculous.

472
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:05,341
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it doesn't make sense.

473
00:41:05,361 --> 00:41:05,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

474
00:41:06,101 --> 00:41:08,002
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's, it's, eh...

475
00:41:09,892 --> 00:41:11,813
[SPEAKER_03]: Really, you kind of start from the other way around.

476
00:41:11,873 --> 00:41:13,754
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you don't even see the assumption.

477
00:41:14,735 --> 00:41:16,856
[SPEAKER_03]: And you might be told that this is what you're assuming.

478
00:41:17,876 --> 00:41:24,820
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you're just thinking in kind of logical terms, it's like, no, no, not like, how would I be doing that?

479
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:27,561
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's it's actually what you imply.

480
00:41:28,902 --> 00:41:36,246
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, any any time you bring yourself back, you realize, like, wait, this is, like, how is how is I overlooking this?

481
00:41:36,546 --> 00:41:38,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's completely obvious in a way.

482
00:41:38,747 --> 00:41:38,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But

483
00:41:39,272 --> 00:41:41,253
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, you don't get there by thinking about it.

484
00:41:41,633 --> 00:41:45,055
[SPEAKER_03]: You get there by doing it, or by not doing it.

485
00:41:45,075 --> 00:41:47,016
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so that's the important point.

486
00:41:47,036 --> 00:41:55,581
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you will have to think about, when you lose it, you will have to, let's say, attend to your idea of what the body is.

487
00:41:56,262 --> 00:42:06,928
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you've received right instructions, and you, to some extent, have some sense of where to look, that idea should not become secondary, because you actually discern the present

488
00:42:09,117 --> 00:42:16,922
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the other thing I was going to say.

489
00:42:17,322 --> 00:42:27,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Once that becomes established, that's the whole point of the... when the description of satipaṭṭhāna says, within the body, he sees the body.

490
00:42:27,989 --> 00:42:29,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the feeling, he sees the feeling.

491
00:42:29,490 --> 00:42:31,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the mind, he sees the mind.

492
00:42:31,891 --> 00:42:33,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Within phenomena, he sees phenomena.

493
00:42:34,153 --> 00:42:37,455
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... you will have your mind established,

494
00:42:38,945 --> 00:42:45,652
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, effortlessly, like, independent of your direct attention on one of these things.

495
00:42:46,793 --> 00:42:52,199
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you will naturally be wanting to have thoughts about those things.

496
00:42:52,639 --> 00:42:54,541
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll want to be, like, clarifying.

497
00:42:55,222 --> 00:42:56,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, what is that feeling?

498
00:42:56,163 --> 00:42:56,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And is it

499
00:42:57,574 --> 00:42:58,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Did it come from here?

500
00:42:58,515 --> 00:42:59,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Did it come from there?

501
00:42:59,876 --> 00:43:04,141
[SPEAKER_00]: As Sutta says, is it made by somebody else?

502
00:43:04,381 --> 00:43:05,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it made by me?

503
00:43:05,643 --> 00:43:06,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it random?

504
00:43:06,484 --> 00:43:07,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it made by both?

505
00:43:08,686 --> 00:43:09,747
[SPEAKER_00]: What will I be tomorrow?

506
00:43:10,148 --> 00:43:11,309
[SPEAKER_00]: What will it be tomorrow?

507
00:43:11,369 --> 00:43:12,270
[SPEAKER_00]: What will it be later?

508
00:43:15,311 --> 00:43:19,876
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole point is, within the presence of that feeling that's there, you see all of this.

509
00:43:20,177 --> 00:43:22,039
[SPEAKER_00]: You see all of these ideas about feelings.

510
00:43:23,360 --> 00:43:27,425
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you... then that's how you will transcend any views that you have in regard to it.

511
00:43:27,505 --> 00:43:30,068
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you will see, wait, this actually comes first.

512
00:43:30,088 --> 00:43:35,915
[SPEAKER_03]: Whether I say that it's made by me or it's made by somebody else, this is what comes first.

513
00:43:40,607 --> 00:43:46,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you start to then imply, like, the feeling is the thing that will come from clarifying these ideas.

514
00:43:46,911 --> 00:43:48,752
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of the assumption that you have.

515
00:43:48,772 --> 00:43:50,373
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the thing you're building.

516
00:43:50,393 --> 00:43:50,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

517
00:43:50,893 --> 00:43:54,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, I will arrive at the feeling by clarifying these ideas.

518
00:43:55,416 --> 00:43:57,357
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but that's where everyone starts from.

519
00:43:57,497 --> 00:43:57,998
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

520
00:43:58,018 --> 00:43:59,699
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what I said.

521
00:44:00,039 --> 00:44:04,342
[SPEAKER_03]: But you have to, first, like, when you haven't seen it, you need to use these ideas.

522
00:44:05,328 --> 00:44:10,929
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you all, at the same time, need to be aware, like, okay, these are just kind of to point me towards where it is.

523
00:44:11,469 --> 00:44:18,151
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you're there, you might still have, well, very likely, will still have the tendency to think, like, all the ideas will help me.

524
00:44:18,331 --> 00:44:19,971
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to keep, keep going, keep going.

525
00:44:20,511 --> 00:44:31,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But no, it's like, right now, if you already established it, now you need to shift the order and see, okay, sure, I still have these ideas, but I need to now put this thing first that I have recognized.

526
00:44:33,698 --> 00:44:35,559
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean that those ideas need to disappear.

527
00:44:35,739 --> 00:44:36,200
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

528
00:44:36,300 --> 00:44:38,441
[SPEAKER_03]: And actually, that's how the ideas would become more accurate.

529
00:44:38,521 --> 00:44:38,822
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

530
00:44:38,982 --> 00:44:46,647
[SPEAKER_03]: Because then, they would be coming now from the right order, rather than just, kind of, piling up information together.

531
00:44:46,747 --> 00:44:47,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

532
00:44:48,728 --> 00:45:02,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that what's meant in some of the suttas, where it talks about, well, for instance, like the bodhisattva thinking, oh, well, all of this thinking is tiring, for example?

533
00:45:04,244 --> 00:45:06,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, no, I don't think it's that exact.

534
00:45:06,646 --> 00:45:09,187
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's more the conceivings that the suttas talk about.

535
00:45:13,790 --> 00:45:14,310
[SPEAKER_02]: I think I'm...

536
00:45:16,251 --> 00:45:24,676
[SPEAKER_02]: I forget exactly which suttas it is that talk about this, but... Majjhima 19, where it says, like, he's been cultivating thoughts of renunciation.

537
00:45:25,097 --> 00:45:29,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Right, yeah, where he kind of realizes, like, this isn't necessary anymore.

538
00:45:30,060 --> 00:45:30,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

539
00:45:30,960 --> 00:45:32,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a slightly different...

540
00:45:32,552 --> 00:45:33,353
[SPEAKER_01]: It's phenomenal now.

541
00:45:33,373 --> 00:45:38,677
[SPEAKER_01]: This is in the context of jhāna and seeing that there's something more refined than even having the thinking and pondering.

542
00:45:38,938 --> 00:45:44,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But these conceivings, not mistaking ideas of body, feeling, mind,

543
00:45:45,690 --> 00:45:53,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And phenomena for those establishments that have to be there, regardless of which ideas you have, that's itself a different dhamma.

544
00:45:53,660 --> 00:45:57,124
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, actually, that's what he...

545
00:45:57,865 --> 00:46:04,713
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, it says there, he became established in jhāna when he realized these phenomena are there.

546
00:46:06,016 --> 00:46:16,740
[SPEAKER_00]: It explicitly says that, and people overlook that completely, and they think, oh, he became established in jhāna, means like he became perfectly focused on one single phenomenon.

547
00:46:16,780 --> 00:46:26,123
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, it says he, after he was building up these thoughts of renunciation and planning his mind in that direction, he said, we realized this is not necessary anymore.

548
00:46:26,724 --> 00:46:30,525
[SPEAKER_03]: That doesn't mean he undid those phenomena and went elsewhere.

549
00:46:31,087 --> 00:46:37,310
[SPEAKER_03]: It means he kind of established himself in a more fundamental intention.

550
00:46:37,690 --> 00:46:45,654
[SPEAKER_00]: His intention was just to see, okay, these phenomena are there, instead of just trying to kind of keep doing them, so to speak.

551
00:46:46,435 --> 00:46:50,477
[SPEAKER_00]: The renunciation, the intention of renunciation, the wholesome mind,

552
00:46:51,303 --> 00:46:51,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there.

553
00:46:52,084 --> 00:46:53,245
[SPEAKER_00]: I have cultivated it already.

554
00:46:53,285 --> 00:46:54,386
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to keep building it.

555
00:46:55,627 --> 00:47:01,674
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when he shifted on to, let's say, proper satipaṭṭhāna.

556
00:47:01,714 --> 00:47:10,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, in fact, many of the suttas would say, in order to develop satipaṭṭhāna, you first start by abandoning the five hindrances.

557
00:47:10,202 --> 00:47:12,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Because those are the ones that are going to be obstructing your

558
00:47:13,572 --> 00:47:18,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity and making you conceive, even if you have the perfect intention not to do so.

559
00:47:18,676 --> 00:47:23,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Conceive the ideas about the body for the present body that underlies those ideas.

560
00:47:24,241 --> 00:47:32,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you've done away with the hindrances, which is what he was doing, like thinking when thoughts of sensuality would arise, he would think about the danger and all that.

561
00:47:33,810 --> 00:47:37,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he was able to, almost effortlessly, have this clarity.

562
00:47:38,394 --> 00:47:39,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, phenomena are there.

563
00:47:43,590 --> 00:47:48,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Because satipaṭṭhāna, in a way, is already the basis of samādhi.

564
00:47:50,535 --> 00:47:54,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So to say, like, it's the nimittas, the grounds of samādhi.

565
00:47:55,258 --> 00:47:56,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not like it's two separate things.

566
00:47:56,979 --> 00:47:59,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I cultivate satipaṭṭhāna first, and then samādhi comes.

567
00:47:59,861 --> 00:48:00,021
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

568
00:48:00,381 --> 00:48:06,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able to have perspective over the body is already a level of samādhi that you have.

569
00:48:07,085 --> 00:48:09,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Might not be the first jhāna, but it's already

570
00:48:10,294 --> 00:48:12,337
[SPEAKER_00]: That aspect, it's a unification.

571
00:48:12,578 --> 00:48:23,097
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is unified in that background thing and isn't as much kind of scattered among the content that still arises.

